---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      There is no single ideal weight, which is why this tool shows several. The classic formulas,
      Devine, Robinson, Miller, and Hamwi, each estimate a target weight from your height and sex, and
      they disagree by a few kilograms because they were built at different times with different numbers.
      Alongside them sits the modern BMI healthy range for your height. Read the formula average as a
      central anchor and the BMI range as the realistic band around it, and pick a point inside that
      band that suits your build.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The Ideal Weight Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How the Formulas Work <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Every classic ideal-weight formula shares the same shape: a base weight for someone five feet
      tall, plus a fixed amount added for each inch above that. Devine, the best known, uses 50 kg for
      men and 45.5 kg for women, adding 2.3 kg per inch. The others simply pick different bases and
      per-inch amounts, which is why their answers spread out. None of them uses anything beyond height
      and sex, so they are fast to compute but blunt: two people the same height get the same estimate
      regardless of frame or muscle.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="why-they-disagree">
    <h2>Why They Disagree <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The formulas were created for different eras and uses, several of them to help calculate
      medication doses rather than to define fitness. Hamwi, from the 1960s, tends to sit highest;
      Miller, from the 1980s, tends to sit lowest; Devine and Robinson land in between. Seeing all four
      at once is the point. A single number pretends to a precision that does not exist, while a spread
      of a few kilograms tells the truth: your target is approximate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="use-the-range">
    <h2>Use the Range, Not the Point <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The modern approach skips the single-number formulas in favour of the BMI healthy-weight range,
      which spans every weight giving a BMI between 18.5 and 24.9 at your height. It is deliberately
      wide because healthy bodies come in different builds. Any weight inside that band is reasonable,
      and where you aim within it depends on your frame, muscle, and how you feel and perform, not on a
      formula from decades ago.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="A band, not a bullseye">
      <p>Treat the formula average as a rough anchor and the BMI healthy range as your real target zone. Chasing one exact number ignores that healthy weight naturally varies with build.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Treating one number as the target">
      <p>
        Locking onto a single formula figure and grinding toward it to the kilogram ignores that the
        formulas themselves disagree by several kilograms. Aim for the healthy range instead.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Ignoring muscle and frame">
      <p>
        Because the formulas see only height and sex, a muscular or broad-framed person will look
        overweight by them while being perfectly healthy. Pair the estimate with a body fat check.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Track toward the range">
      <p>Once you have a target zone, follow your weight trend toward it over weeks rather than reacting to daily swings. Everything here runs in your browser and never leaves your device.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
